  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nStrong summer subtropical ridge was centered over the\nMississippi valley at midday, with southwest midlevel flow aloft\nover SW KS. Satellite imagery indicated the eastern periphery of\nthe monsoon circulation over SW KS, with mid and high clouds\nnorthwest of Dodge City. Radar indicated a few sprinkles\nembedded in this monsoon plume across the northwest zones. \nCAMs are in good agreement that scattered thunderstorms will\nredevelop in this moisture plume after 4 pm, favoring these\nnorthwest zones. Primary risk in the hot, mixed boundary layer\nenvironment will be outflow winds of 50-70 mph, per SPC 5-15%\nwind probability for northern zones. High based thunderstorms\nshould focus along I-70 Monday evening, and NBM correctly has\nits higher pops here. \n\nElsewhere, strong southwest winds will continue through sunset,\ngusting 35-45 mph. Downslope compression will send temperatures\nto near the records for September 14 (100 at DDC). \n\nFortunately, Monday's heat will be short lived, as a strong cold\nfront will advance through SW KS Tuesday. With the cold front's\ntiming during the diurnal heating cycle, a large temperature\nrange will unfold Tuesday, from 60s and 70s north, to near 100\nsoutheast. Cold front is expected to enter northern zones at\nsunrise, and exit Barber county by 7 pm. Exact high temperatures\nfor each location will depend of frontal timing, and a non-\ndiurnal curve downward is expected behind the front with\nelevated northeast winds. 12z GFS shows decent cold advection\nfor September, with 850 mb temperatures falling through the\nteens C. Increasing and lowering midlevel clouds will also add\nto the fall-like feel Tuesday afternoon. \n\nScattered thunderstorms will develop along/behind the advancing\ncold front Tuesday afternoon/evening, favoring the southeast\nzones. Shear and instability will both be modest at best, but\nstronger storm clusters will be capable of outflow wind gusts,\nper SPC marginal 5% wind probability southeast zones. Cold \nfront will begin retreating northward quickly Tuesday night, \nwith winds quickly returning to the south through Wednesday. \nThis warm advection process should encourage at least scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms with locally heavy rainfall probable\nthrough Tuesday night.\n\nNBM reloads above normal temperatures back into the lower 90s as\nsoon as Wednesday, but much global model guidance keeps SW KS in\nthe 80s. Higher confidence in the heat returning Thursday, well\ninto the 90s, along with a dry forecast, as the western\nperiphery of the subtropical ridge retrogrades westward onto the\nhigh plains. The heat will continue Friday afternoon, before the\nnext strong cold front arrives next weekend. MEX and NBM\nguidance suggests some locations will remain in the 70s Sunday\nafternoon.",
